## Named CPU PNG preview/final quality controls (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native `atrament-paper` command now supports an explicit PNG-only
`--quality preview|final` selection: preview uses 72 DPI and final uses the
existing 150-DPI default. This changes only raster sampling resolution, not
page geometry, physical pen marks, transparency, PDF vector ink or the saved
paper profile. Existing callers retain the 150-DPI default and explicit
`--dpi 72..300` option unchanged.

Quality and DPI are mutually exclusive. Duplicate or unknown quality names
and use without `--format png` refuse before output, and both named profiles
still enforce the 5-megapixel raster limit and 24 MB output cap. A native
regression compares each named output byte-for-byte to its already-tested
explicit-DPI equivalent and checks invalid/oversized requests.

The `atrament-profiles notebook` authored-ink exporter also accepts these
same PNG-only `--quality preview|final` choices, with its prior 150-DPI
default and explicit 72..300 DPI range unchanged. Selected page-two native
RGBA PNG with an actual user-authored stroke is byte-identical to the
matching `--dpi 72` and `--dpi 150` output. Unknown, repeated, PDF-only and
DPI-conflicting quality selections refuse before replacing existing output.

Both paths still enforce the same physical page and raster budgets. A
real CLI subprocess additionally exports exact saved glyph-library ink in
both quality modes and checks the selected output remains unchanged for
conflicting quality/DPI and non-PNG requests.

Preview and final are bounded CPU raster resolutions, not independently
calibrated paper/pigment looks. The accepted feature still needs CPU time
and memory measurements, line-art extraction with a loss report and print
scan correction before the broader task can close.

## Reproducible native CPU cost evidence (partial) — 2026-11-04

On Linux, the following command runs built native Rust executables directly,
*outside Cargo startup*, using GNU `/usr/bin/time`:

```sh
node --test tests/backend/render-performance-evidence/domain/lib.mjs
```
It reports four independent `CPU_PNG_MEASUREMENT` JSON observations:
72-DPI Preview and 150-DPI Final for A4 grid paper, and the same pair for
preprinted A4 containing one caller-authored colored pen segment. Each
observation records CPU model/architecture, wall/user/system seconds, peak
resident memory in KiB, raster dimensions, PNG byte size and SHA-256.

One local x64 Linux run on an Intel Xeon E5-2690 v3 reported:

| Native output | Pixels | Wall seconds | Peak RSS (KiB) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Grid Preview | 596 × 842 | 0.20 | 10,552 |
| Grid Final | 1241 × 1754 | 0.86 | 36,944 |
| Authored ink Preview | 596 × 842 | 0.20 | 11,412 |
| Authored ink Final | 1241 × 1754 | 0.85 | 37,568 |

The test verifies actual transparent PNG bytes, distinct preview/final
raster dimensions, and preservation of an existing output file when A3 at
300 DPI breaches the native five-megapixel limit. It asserts **no**
wall-time or memory threshold: these single machine-specific observations
are not a product performance budget, a GPU-absence audit, or evidence for
unimplemented image/mathematics/zoom workloads. The native CPU rendering
path is measured; build time, JS harness and PNG analysis are excluded from
the external GNU-time observation.

This is partial measurable-cost evidence only. Transparent line-art
extraction with an explicit loss report, print/scan correction and broader
representative workload benchmarks remain unimplemented; P5-015 stays open.

